        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="RmEEv3Yl5RT3NWJRKuHY-91" value="&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 35px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font&gt;Pod Network&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/span&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;resizable=0;points=[];autosize=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=35;fontFamily=Architects Daughter;fontSource=https%3A%2F%2Ffonts.googleapis.com%2Fcss%3Ffamily%3DArchitects%2BDaughter;strokeWidth=3;fontColor=#4D9900;fontStyle=1" parent="WfwI8hsEHnIwHEpeUaaj-79" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1490" y="280" width="240" height="60" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="RmEEv3Yl5RT3NWJRKuHY-119" value="&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;background-color: rgb(153, 255, 153); font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;Azure CNI Key Design Points:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;Azure CNI (Container Networking Interface) is a networking solution for Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) that integrates with Azure Virtual Network (VNet). It allows pods to receive IP addresses from the Azure VNet, enabling seamless communication between pods and other resources within the VNet.&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 102);&quot;&gt;Use Case:&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;Azure CNI is ideal for scenarios where you need:&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 153);&quot;&gt;Direct Communication&lt;/span&gt;: Pods need to communicate directly with other Azure resources (e.g., VMs, databases) within the same VNet without NAT.&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;Network Security: Enhanced security through Azure VNet features like Network Security Groups (NSGs) and Azure Firewall.&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 153);&quot;&gt;Scalability&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 153);&quot;&gt;Limited by the number of IP addresses available in the VNet subnet.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Requires careful planning of IP address space to avoid exhaustion.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;In Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) with Azure CNI, the LoadBalancer type Service can directly &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 153);&quot;&gt;route traffic to the pods without needing a NodePort.&lt;/span&gt; However, the NodePort is still created as part of the Service configuration for several reasons:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;br style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 29px;&quot;&gt;Fallback Mechanism: If the load balancer fails or is misconfigured, the NodePort provides an alternative way to access the Service. You can access the Service using &amp;lt;NodeIP&amp;gt;:&amp;lt;NodePort&amp;gt;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=29;fontFamily=Courier New;html=1;" parent="WfwI8hsEHnIwHEpeUaaj-79" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-580" y="1020" width="2090" height="790" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="YLipig3j6BaiT96UUwU8-10" value="&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(128, 255, 0);&quot;&gt;Use Case:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;AKS CNI is ideal for scenarios where:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Direct Communication: Pods need to communicate directly with other Azure resources (e.g., VMs, databases) within the same VNet without NAT.&lt;br&gt;Network Security: Enhanced security through Azure VNet features like Network Security Groups (NSGs) and Azure Firewall.&lt;br&gt;Scalability: Large-scale deployments with high pod density and complex networking requirements.&lt;br&gt;Advanced Networking: Applications require advanced networking features such as custom DNS, private link, and service endpoints.&lt;br&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(128, 255, 0);&quot;&gt;Key Design Points:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;VNet Integration:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Pods receive IP addresses from the Azure VNet subnet, allowing direct communication with other resources in the VNet.&lt;br&gt;Simplifies network management by using familiar Azure networking tools and policies.&lt;br&gt;Service CIDR and DNS:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Separate CIDR block for Kubernetes services (--service-cidr 10.101.0.0/16).&lt;br&gt;Specific IP reserved for the DNS service (--dns-service-ip 10.101.0.10).&lt;br&gt;Managed Identity:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Uses Azure Managed Identity for secure access to Azure resources (--enable-managed-identity).&lt;br&gt;Assigns a user-assigned identity for additional security (--assign-identity).&lt;br&gt;Add-ons and Integrations:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Enables monitoring add-on for cluster insights (--enable-addons monitoring).&lt;br&gt;Integrates with Azure Application Gateway for ingress (-a ingress-appgw --appgw-name myApplicationGateway --appgw-subnet-id $APP_GW_SUBNET_ID).&lt;br&gt;Scalability and Performance:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Configures the maximum number of pods per node (--max-pods 30).&lt;br&gt;Ensures high availability with multiple node pools and replicas.&lt;br&gt;Deployment and Service Management:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Deploys applications and services within namespaces (k create ns demo-ns).&lt;br&gt;Manages external and internal load balancers for service exposure (k apply -f service-internal-lb.yaml and k apply -f service-external-lb.yaml)." style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=40;fontFamily=Architects Daughter;html=1;" parent="WfwI8hsEHnIwHEpeUaaj-79" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="-3810" y="350" width="2240" height="1740" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="oLPVPFHUQ2T455JdV-RN-5" value="&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 55px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 153);&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;AKS Azure CNI&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;resizable=0;points=[];autosize=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=35;fontFamily=Architects Daughter;fontSource=https%3A%2F%2Ffonts.googleapis.com%2Fcss%3Ffamily%3DArchitects%2BDaughter;" parent="WfwI8hsEHnIwHEpeUaaj-79" vertex="1">
