        <mxCell id="RmEEv3Yl5RT3NWJRKuHY-102" value="&lt;div&gt;Cluster IP: 10.101.19.231 (internal IP within the cluster)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;LoadBalancer Ingress: 172.16.240.91 (internal IP assigned by the load balancer)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Ports:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Port: 8080/TCP (internal port)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;TargetPort: 80/TCP (port on the selected pods)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;NodePort: 32305/TCP (port on each node that routes to the Service)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Endpoints: IPs and ports of the selected pods (172.16.240.31:80, 172.16.240.4:80, 172.16.240.79:80)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Session Affinity: None (no session affinity)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;External Traffic Policy: Cluster (traffic is balanced across all nodes in the cluster)&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Events: Logs related to ensuring the load balancer&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Role of NodePort:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Even though this is an internal load balancer, the NodePort (32305/TCP) is created by default. It provides an alternative access method for internal traffic within the cluster, allowing access to the Service via &amp;lt;NodeIP&amp;gt;:32305. This can be useful for internal debugging, testing, or fallback mechanisms.&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;textShadow=0;fontFamily=Courier New;fontSize=38;labelBackgroundColor=none;html=1;" parent="RmEEv3Yl5RT3NWJRKuHY-92" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="6240" y="1150" width="1430" height="980" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
